[{"code":"refutable_let_pattern","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/parse/bad/RefutableLetPattern.beni","start":{"line":12,"col":5},"end":{"line":12,"col":6}},"title":"REFUTABLE PATTERN","message":"I found `1` in a `let` pattern, but a `let` pattern must always match.\n\nA literal and a list, with a spread or without, each match some values of their\ntype and not others, whatever that type turns out to be, so neither can be bound\nin a `let`. A\nCONSTRUCTOR can, when its type has only that one — `(Box n) = b` is fine and\n`(Just n) = m` is not, and I say which after I have checked the types. To\nmatch on more than one shape, use `case`."},{"code":"refutable_let_pattern","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/parse/bad/RefutableLetPattern.beni","start":{"line":18,"col":5},"end":{"line":18,"col":6}},"title":"REFUTABLE PATTERN","message":"I found `[` in a `let` pattern, but a `let` pattern must always match.\n\nA literal and a list, with a spread or without, each match some values of their\ntype and not others, whatever that type turns out to be, so neither can be bound\nin a `let`. A\nCONSTRUCTOR can, when its type has only that one — `(Box n) = b` is fine and\n`(Just n) = m` is not, and I say which after I have checked the types. To\nmatch on more than one shape, use `case`."},{"code":"refutable_let_pattern","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/parse/bad/RefutableLetPattern.beni","start":{"line":24,"col":5},"end":{"line":24,"col":6}},"title":"REFUTABLE PATTERN","message":"I found `[` in a `let` pattern, but a `let` pattern must always match.\n\nA literal and a list, with a spread or without, each match some values of their\ntype and not others, whatever that type turns out to be, so neither can be bound\nin a `let`. A\nCONSTRUCTOR can, when its type has only that one — `(Box n) = b` is fine and\n`(Just n) = m` is not, and I say which after I have checked the types. To\nmatch on more than one shape, use `case`."},{"code":"refutable_parameter_pattern","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/parse/bad/RefutableLetPattern.beni","start":{"line":35,"col":5},"end":{"line":35,"col":6}},"title":"REFUTABLE PARAMETER","message":"I found `[` in a parameter, but a parameter must always match.\n\nA literal and a list, with a spread or without, each match some arguments and\nnot others, whatever their type turns out to be, and there is nowhere for the\nrest to go. (A CONSTRUCTOR is allowed here when its type has only that one; I\nsay which after I have checked the types.) Take the argument whole and `case` on\nit:\n\n    un m =\n        case m of\n            Just n ->\n                n\n\n            Nothing ->\n                0"}]
